C = [
("h1", "9. What Paul Says of Signs in His Own Letters"),
("p", "Claim: in the sentences of Paul’s letters read here, each states whose signs they are and where they were done. He says that the Jews require a sign, that Christ has wrought signs by him among the Gentiles, that the signs of an apostle were wrought among the Corinthians, and that the one who ministers the Spirit to the Galatians worketh miracles among them. None of these sentences is a command to seek signs or a promise of signs to every believer. The passages on gifts and tongues in 1 Corinthians 12 to 14 are read in Volumes 2 and 3. The letters also record the sickness of Paul’s fellow workers without recording a healing. The volume reads the sentences and the silences."),
("sub", "9.1 The Jews require a sign"),
("p", "Paul writes to Corinth: “For the Jews require a sign, and the Greeks seek after wisdom: But we preach Christ crucified, unto the Jews a stumblingblock, and unto the Greeks foolishness; But unto them which are called, both Jews and Greeks, Christ the power of God, and the wisdom of God” (1 Corinthians 1:22–24). The sentence is a statement about Jews. “The Jews require a sign.” It does not say that the Corinthians require one. Paul’s answer to the demand is a person and a message, “Christ crucified,” and the call is to “them which are called, both Jews and Greeks.”"),
("p", "The verse is read with the Gospels. The Lord met the same demand in Israel and gave the sign of Jonah (chapter 5). Paul names the demand as a feature of Israel and sets his preaching against it. Acts Commentary Volume 2 reads the verse in the setting of the Corinthian letter."),
("sub", "9.2 Signs and wonders among the Gentiles"),
("p", "In Romans Paul gives an account of his own work. “For I will not dare to speak of any of those things which Christ hath not wrought by me, to make the Gentiles obedient, by word and deed, Through mighty signs and wonders, by the power of the Spirit of God; so that from Jerusalem, and round about unto Illyricum, I have fully preached the gospel of Christ” (Romans 15:18, 19). The sentence names the agent, “Christ,” the instrument, “me,” and the place, the arc from Jerusalem to Illyricum. It names the ground of the work, “by word and deed,” and the means, “mighty signs and wonders, by the power of the Spirit of God.” It is a statement about what Christ wrought by Paul. Gospel Commentary Volume 2 reads the passage with the Lord’s ministry to Israel."),
("p", "Paul also appeals to the Galatians: “He therefore that ministereth to you the Spirit, and worketh miracles among you, doeth he it by the works of the law, or by the hearing of faith?” (Galatians 3:5). The verbs “ministereth” and “worketh” are in the present tense, and they describe the one who gives the Spirit and works miracles among the Galatians. The sentence asks a question about the source. It does not command anyone to seek miracles, and it does not promise them. It does not say how long the miracles continue or who works them through whom."),
("sub", "9.3 The signs of an apostle"),
("p", "To the Corinthians Paul writes, “Truly the signs of an apostle were wrought among you in all patience, in signs, and wonders, and mighty deeds” (2 Corinthians 12:12). The sentence calls the signs “the signs of an apostle,” and it uses the past tense, “were wrought.” It joins three words, signs, wonders and mighty deeds, that the earlier chapters traced. Volume 4 of this series reads the verse with the offices of apostle and prophet, and the volume leaves the argument to that place."),
("sub", "9.4 The silence about healing in the letters"),
("p", "The letters record the illness of Paul’s companions in plain terms. “Yet I supposed it necessary to send to you Epaphroditus, my brother, and companion in labour, and fellowsoldier, but your messenger, and he that ministered to my wants. For he longed after you all, and was full of heaviness, because that ye had heard that he had been sick. For indeed he was sick nigh unto death: but God had mercy on him; and not on him only, but on me also, lest I should have sorrow upon sorrow” (Philippians 2:25–27). Paul writes to Timothy, “Drink no longer water, but use a little wine for thy stomach’s sake and thine often infirmities” (1 Timothy 5:23), and in a later letter he says, “Erastus abode at Corinth: but Trophimus have I left at Miletum sick” (2 Timothy 4:20)."),
("p", "The three passages state a fact about a person and say what Paul did or advised. In Philippians, God had mercy. In the first letter to Timothy, wine is advised for the stomach. In the second, Trophimus is left sick at Miletum. No passage says that a healing was attempted, that a sign was done or that a sign was expected. The passages do not say why. The volume does not supply a reason, and it does not set the three passages against the healings of Acts. It records that the letters mention illness among Paul’s fellow workers and that none of the three reports a miracle."),
("p", "Volume 3 of the series takes up healing and the Body in full. This chapter makes only the observation that, in the three letters, sickness is mentioned and handled in ordinary terms."),
("sub", "9.5 Paul’s other uses of the word"),
("p", "Paul uses the word “sign” in three further places, and each belongs to the uses traced in chapters 2 and 4. In Romans he says of Abraham, “And he received the sign of circumcision, a seal of the righteousness of the faith which he had yet being uncircumcised” (Romans 4:11). The sign is the covenant sign of Genesis 17:11, and Paul names it by the word chapter 2 traced. At the close of his second letter to Thessalonica he writes, “The salutation of Paul with mine own hand, which is the token in every epistle: so I write” (2 Thessalonians 3:17). The token is his own handwriting, a mark by which the letters are known."),
("p", "The third place is the sentence about the coming of the lawless one: “Even him, whose coming is after the working of Satan with all power and signs and lying wonders, And with all deceivableness of unrighteousness in them that perish; because they received not the love of the truth, that they might be saved” (2 Thessalonians 2:9, 10). The passage uses the same three words, power, signs and wonders, for what Satan works. It is read beside Deuteronomy 13:1–3, which allows that a sign may come to pass in the service of a message that turns from the LORD. The passage does not name who the man is or when he comes; Study 74 reads it beside the other final-ruler passages. Here it carries one point: the words of the vocabulary do not by themselves settle whose work the sign is."),
("ctbl", "Signs in Paul’s letters.", [
    ("Passage", "What Paul says", "What the sentence leaves unsaid"),
    ("1 Corinthians 1:22–24", "The Jews require a sign; we preach Christ crucified", "That the Corinthians or the Body require one"),
    ("Romans 15:18, 19", "Christ wrought mighty signs and wonders by Paul among the Gentiles", "That the Body is promised signs"),
    ("Galatians 3:5", "The one who ministers the Spirit worketh miracles among the Galatians; by the law or by faith?", "Any command to seek them; how long they continue"),
    ("2 Corinthians 12:12", "The signs of an apostle were wrought among you", "That anyone but an apostle has them"),
    ("Romans 4:11; 2 Thessalonians 2:9, 10; 3:17", "The sign of circumcision; the signs of the lawless one; the token of Paul’s hand", "That every use of the word is a miracle"),
    ("Philippians 2:25–27; 1 Timothy 5:23; 2 Timothy 4:20", "Sickness among fellow workers", "Any healing sign"),
  ], [0.24, 0.40, 0.36]),
("sub", "What the volume takes from the passages"),
("bul", [
    "In the sentences read here, signs are something done, and none is a command or a promise to every believer.",
    "He says the Jews require a sign and answers with Christ crucified.",
    "He calls the signs wrought among the Corinthians “the signs of an apostle” and puts the verb in the past.",
    "His other uses of the word are the sign of circumcision, the signs of the lawless one and the token of his own hand.",
    "His letters record sickness among his companions without recording a healing.",
    "The passages on gifts and tongues in 1 Corinthians 12 to 14 are read in Volumes 2 and 3.",
  ]),
]
